# Dev / Test Servers (portless, HTTPS, LAN)

Whenever the user says "start the dev server" or "start the test server":
never hand them a raw `host:port`. Serve everything through portless in
LAN mode and give the `https://<app>.local/…` address, reachable from this
machine and every device on the LAN/tailnet. Prefer the same single-host
model as prod: UI + API on ONE host — no separate API hostname. The app's
routes (`/`, `/settings`, …) work identically in dev and prod.

## One-time setup

- `npm install -g portless`, `portless trust` (local CA in system store)
- Browser trust for other devices: install `~/.portless/ca.pem` as a
  trusted CA on the device (Android: Settings → Security → Install from
  storage; iOS: install profile + enable full trust). First visit warns
  until then.

## Start (exact commands)

```bash
# 1. proxy with LAN mode: binds 0.0.0.0:443 (needs sudo once) so .local
#    names work across the network. loopback-only without --lan.
portless proxy stop && portless proxy start --lan

# 2. register memorable names (NOT <name>.localhost — portless publishes
#    those to mDNS as <name>.localhost.local, which nobody can remember).
#    single-host: point <app>.local at the dev-server (e.g. Vite) port.
#    The dev server proxies /api + /ws to the backend, and prod's server
#    serves UI + API on one port too, so the test URL mirrors prod.
portless alias <app>.local <dev-server-port>
```

## Canonical dev/test names (mirror prod: one host)

- UI = API host: `https://<app>.local/` plus the app's own routes —
  exactly like prod's single origin.
- No separate `<app>api.local`: the dev server proxies `/api` + `/ws` to
  the backend.
- Aliases live in `~/.portless` and survive proxy restarts; re-register
  them whenever the underlying ports change.

## SPA / client-side routes

- Deep links and refresh must serve `index.html` with a 200. If the
  backend uses a static-file `not_found_service` fallback it may return
  the index body but keep a 404 status — fine for browsers, but `curl`
  checks should expect 200 only on explicitly registered routes.

## Gotchas (learned the hard way)

- Vite 5 has no `--allowedHosts` CLI flag (v6+ does). For `.local`
  hostnames add `allowedHosts: true` under `server:` in the Vite config
  (temp test edit — revert before commit).
- Dev-server `server.proxy` `/api` + `/ws` targets must point at the
  *backend* port in use; fix before starting it or API calls 404/stall.
- No dedicated `/health` route is common — a 404 there often means the
  server *is* answering. Health-check against a real API endpoint instead.
- Orca's embedded browser (Electron) does not trust the portless CA even
  after `~/.pki/nssdb` install → blank page. Verify via `curl -sk` and
  the user's own system-Chromium browser instead.
- `.localhost` names resolve only on this machine; `.local` needs avahi
  on the server side and mDNS working on the client (Android is flaky —
  fall back to `https://<server-host>:<port>` via MagicDNS).
- Portless cert SANs cover `<name>.local` + `*.local`. `openssl s_client
  -connect <name>.local:443 -servername <name>.local` verifies the chain.
- Revert to loopback-only: `portless proxy stop && portless proxy start`
  (no `--lan`).
